{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01091 - MONDO_1461",
  "verbalization": "Butenafine is associated with the treatment of dermatophytosis of the groin and perianal area, which is a fungal infection caused by dermatophytes. This condition is also indicated to be treated by Sulconazole, Econazole, and Oxiconazole, all of which are antifungal agents. The evidence suggests that Butenafine may be repurposed for the treatment of Tinea Corporis, a common fungal infection affecting the skin. The shared indication of dermatophytosis and the related antifungal treatments imply a potential mechanism of action where Butenafine could exert antifungal effects similar to those of the other agents. The integration of these pathways highlights the possibility that Butenafine may act as a viable alternative or complementary treatment for Tinea Corporis, particularly in cases where the efficacy of other antifungals is limited. The evidence supports a direct link between Butenafine and Tinea Corporis through the shared antifungal indication, though further clinical validation would be necessary to confirm this relationship.",
